Z||000000||ROD||05-27-99||12:25 PM||||<br>i have a 87GT. It started to become harder and harder to disenguage the clutch and shift into any gear, at any speed.  If i pumped the pedal or waited a few seconds i could shift again.  The clutch,pressure plate, and pilot bearing have been replaced less than 8,000 miles ago.  I just replaced the slave cylinder and bled the line today. Now i almost no pedal responce whatsoever! If i do get it in gear, it wont come back out.  If i shut the car off i can move the shifter and change gears freely with no problem.  Its obvious to me that the clutch is not disenguaging...the only thing i havnt replaced is the Master Cylinder, it looses no fluid, but im told that doesnt matter it can still be bad. <p>any ideas? i want this thing running again before Memorial weekend<p>thanks for the help
Z||000001||Eric||05-27-99||01:50 PM||||Rod, I had to replace the slave cylinder, master cylinder, and line before my car could retain a good pedal feel.  I think I am a pioneer in the field of line replacement because no one else on this forum has done it.  I would recommend changing the master cylinder first.  It is a few bucks cheaper ($70 vs. $120 for the line).  If you still can't get it right, you should bite the bullet and replace the entire line.  The old flex tubes deteriorate and allow air to get into the system.  I know the Fiero Store makes a near perfect reproduction line with the proper flex tubes.  DO NOT try to fix the flex hose yourself.  You will not be able to find the parts you need to make it seal properly.  And there is another small flex hose under the spare tire shelf you would need to replace too.  Also, find out how to bleed the system completly.  I have been using the V8 Archie method (v8archie.com)and it has worked well.  However, I am going to buy a presure bleeder to make sure there is absolutly no air in the line.  Good luck getting it done by this weekend, I've been working on mine for about two months.
Z||000002||87FieroGT||05-27-99||06:49 PM||||I have had to replace all the components for the clutch system also. Not very fun. But after I did the car has never been better.
Z||000003||ChadMan||05-28-99||07:13 AM||||I'd definitely replace that master cylinder, Rod.  I had a similar problem to yours, took my 88 Coupe to my mechanic and told him to replace all the clutch mechanicals.  He did, and gave me my car back with the same problem, a bill for $750 and the old clutch disc to prove he had done the work - the old disc still had almost 1/2 the friction material left!<p>I took the car home and replaced the master cylinder - $80 and two hours' work and it was good as new.  At least I won't have to screw with my clutch for another 100,000 miles or so.
